<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bob Hawke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bob Hawke was the Labor prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1983 to 1991, after leading the Australian Council of Trade Unions. His government floated the Australian dollar, deregulated finance and lowered tariffs, governed through a prices and incomes accord with the unions, introduced Medicare and initiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-pacific-economic-cooperation">Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation</a>. He is Labor's longest-serving prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bob-hawke.jpg" alt="Bob Hawke" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: © Commonwealth of Australia 2011 · CC BY-SA 3.0 au · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bob_Hawke-1983.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert James Lee Hawke was born on 9 December 1929 in Border Town, South Australia, the second child of Arthur Clem Hawke, a Congregationalist minister, and Edith Emily Hawke, a schoolteacher. He was educated at Perth Modern School and the University of Western Australia, graduating in 1952, and then won a Rhodes Scholarship to University College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he wrote a thesis on wage-fixing in Australia. In 1956 he joined the Australian Council of Trade Unions (ACTU) as a research officer. He became its advocate in the national wages case, won a fifteen-shilling increase in 1959, and in 1969 was elected ACTU president although he had never held elected office in a union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hawke became president of the Labor Party in 1973. In 1980 he stood down from both presidencies and was elected to the House of Representatives for Wills. Three years later he was elected unopposed to replace Bill Hayden as Labor leader and, within five weeks, led it to a landslide victory at the 1983 election. He was sworn in as prime minister on 11 March 1983 and won again in 1984, 1987 and 1990, which made him the most electorally successful prime minister in Labor's history.</p><p>With the ACTU Secretary, Bill Kelty, he negotiated the Prices and Incomes Accord of 1983, under which unions limited wage demands in return for a government guarantee to minimise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and raise the social wage. The government floated the Australian dollar, deregulated the financial system, cut tariffs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, privatised enterprises such as Qantas, and established Medicare. In 1989 Hawke proposed a regional forum that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-pacific-economic-cooperation">Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation</a>, whose first meeting was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> in November 1989.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In June 1991 Hawke faced a leadership challenge from the Treasurer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-keating">Paul Keating</a>, and kept power; Keating challenged again six months later, won narrowly and replaced him. Hawke retired from parliament and worked in business and for charitable causes. He died on 16 May 2019 aged 89 and remains his party's longest-serving prime minister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bob Hawke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
